# Recent advances in perovskite/organic integrated solar cells

Abstract The integrated perovskite/organic solar cell (IPOSC) is widely concerned as an effective approach to broaden the spectrum of perovskite solar cell (PerSC) by utilizing near-infrared light of lower bandgap organic semiconductor. Compared to tandem solar cells, the IPOSCs eliminate the preparation of the intermediate layer and simplify the manufacturing process, but retain the advantages of wide light harvesting. Meanwhile, the IPOSCs can maintain the open-circuit voltage as high as PerSCs. This review summarizes the recent developments of perovskite materials and low-bandgap organic conjugated materials applied in solar cells. Then, the working mechanism of IPOSCs and the recent developments of IPOSCs based on low-bandgap donor and acceptor materials are highlighted. Besides, the study of charge dynamic in IPOSC is summarized. Finally, the potential of IPOSCs and approach to improve device performance are also envisaged.
Keywords Organic solar cell; Perovskite solar cell; Perovskite/organic integrated solar cell; Conjugated molecules
## 1 Introduction
Solar cells are regarded as an important way to solve environmental pollution problems and utilize renewable energy, so various types of solar cells have been developed. Among them, the thin-film solar cells such as perovskite solar cells (PerSCs) and organic solar cells (OSCs) are drawn great attention from researchers due to the advantages of simple manufacture, lightweight, and solution processing. The certified highest power conversion efficiency (PCE) of singlejunction PerSCs and OSCs has reached $25.2\%$ and $17.4\%$ [1]. The PCE of the single-junction PerSCs is approach to that of the monocrystalline silicon solar cells $(26.1\%)$ , which is getting closer to the Shockley-Queisser limit. In order to solve the limitation of thermodynamic principles and thus achieve higher PCE, stacked solar cells are designed. The stacked device is composed of two sub-cells with different absorption spectra. The sub-cell with a wide bandgap serves as the front cell to absorb high-energy light, and the sub-cell with a narrow bandgap serves as the rear cell to absorb low-energy light, thereby realizing a broader spectrum utilization of sunlight [2–4]. The stacked solar cells are divided into seriesstacked (tandem) solar cells and parallel-stacked solar cells. Generally, a tandem solar cell is formed by connecting a front solar cell, a recombination layer and a back solar cell in series. Although the tandem configuration can improve the PCE of solar cells, a recombination layer is required between the two sub-cells, which requires consideration of the chemical and physical characteristics of the sub-cell thus complexing the fabrication of the stacked solar cell. In addition, to achieve the best efficiency, the current density of the front and rear subcells should well-match with each other.
In contrast, the IPOSCs combining perovskite and bulkheterojunction (BHJ) are using orthogonal solvent sequential processing to prepare an organic BHJ on the perovskite without requiring a recombination layer like stacked device [5–7]. This structure simplifies the manufacturing process. Owing to the strong absorption coefficient and high carrier mobility of perovskite materials, the high-energy sunlight can be effectively absorbed by the perovskite active layer. With the synthesis of organic materials with a low-bandgap and high light absorption, the absorption spectra of OSCs have reached $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ [8, 9]. This allows that the organic active layer in IPOSCs can effectively absorb sunlight in near-infrared (NIR) region. Figure 1 shows the absorption spectra distribution of the two absorption layers of IPOSCs. Therefore, the solar cell with perovskite/BHJ integrated structure can employ ultraviolet–visible light and NIR light, thereby increasing the short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of the device.
In this review, the progress of perovskite materials and low-bandgap organic materials will be introduced. Then, the advantages and working mechanism of IPOSCs and the recent development of IPOSCs using low-bandgap donor or acceptor as NIR light absorber will be summarized. The charge dynamic will also be discussed based on existing research. Finally, a brief summarize and prospect will be carried out.
## 2 Working mechanism of IPOSCs
Owing to the orthogonality of the solution for perovskite and organic active layers, the IPOSCs can be obtained by simply spin-coating the organic active layer on perovskite film. These IPOSCs cancel the preparation of the intermediate layer of the stacked structure but still has the function of utilizing broad spectrum of the tandem solar cell. The perovskite layer in IPOSCs absorbs sunlight to directly generate electrons and holes. The BHJ layer first generates excitons after absorbing light due to large binding energy, and then excitons dissociate into electrons and holes. Owing to the bipolar transport and high charge mobility of perovskite materials, the charge recombination is suppressed when the electrons and holes pass through the perovskite layer. The holes in the organic layer are generally transported in the networks formed by the donor polymer, and the electrons are transported in the domains formed by the acceptor material. This characteristic provides the possibility of IPOSCs operation [7, 10, 11]. Owing to more NIR light is employed, the IPOSCs can significantly increase the photocurrent of the devices. Meanwhile, the IPOSCs can also maintain a high voltage for that the open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ in IPOSCs is affected by the perovskite layer [12]. So far, with the efforts of researchers, the IPOSCs have achieved the highest PCE of $20.61\%$ [13].
## 3 Active layer materials in IPOSCs
## 3.1 Perovskite materials
With miniaturization, fraction of free surfaces increases, which has dominated influence on the total mechanical properties of material. Perovskite is a crystalline structure of $\mathrm{AB}\mathrm{X}_{3}$ (Fig. 2a), in which the Position A is a monovalent cation, such as methylammonium (MA), formamidinium (FA), Cs, the Position B is a divalent metal cation, mainly lead and tin and the Position X is a halogen anion [14, 15]. Owing to the long carrier migration distance, adjustable bandgap, solution processing, high absorption coefficient and low exciton binding energy of perovskite materials, the perovskite materials have shown good application prospects in solar cells [16].
Organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite materials $\mathrm{(CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b B r_{3}}$ ) were used by Miyasaka’s group in 2009 as dye sensitizers for dye-sensitized solar cells [17]. The first all-solid-state PerSCs was developed by Kim et al. [18] in 2012. These PerSCs using Spiro-OMeTAD as the holes transport layer and $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ as the light-absorbing layer, achieving a PCE of $9.7\%$ . After that, the research of $\mathrm{PerSCs}$ has been developed rapidly. The $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ possess a bandgap of $1.57\ \mathrm{eV}$ and absorption edge at about $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , indicating that just a small amount of NIR can be utilized in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ based PerSCs. The bandgap of the organic– inorganic halide perovskite is tunable by changing the A, B or $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ site groups. When replacing $\mathrm{I}^{-}$ by $\mathrm{Br}^{-}$ on $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{X}$ site in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ , the $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ exhibits wider bandgap $(2.3~\mathrm{eV})$ and narrower absorption range compared to $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ [19]. Hence, although the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b B r_{3}}$ based PerSCs can achieve higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ , the PCE of it is decreased compared with that of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ based devices. In addition, by replacing $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}^{+}$ with $\mathrm{CH(NH}_{2}\mathrm{)}_{2}+$ and $\mathrm{Cs^{+}}$ , the bandgap of the resulting perovskite material will also change. The $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{CH(NH_{2})_{2}P b I_{3}}$ have feature bandgap of 1.73 and $1.48\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [20]. Owing to the FA cations-based perovskite materials have wider absorption to ensure that the PerSCs can obtain a greater $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , almost all the current highefficiency PerSCs use FA cations-based perovskite materials. However, although $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ broadens the photo-response of $\mathrm{PerSCs}$ to $850~\mathrm{nm}$ , most of the near-infrared lights still cannot be used.
In order to further broaden the absorption spectrum of perovskite, B site replacement was conducted. The $\mathrm{Pb}$ was always replaced by Sn in current research. Since the external electronic structure of Sn is similar to that of $\mathrm{Pb}$ , and the ion radius of $\mathrm{Sn}$ is $0.118~\mathrm{nm}$ and is similar to that of Pb $(0.119~\mathrm{nm})$ , indicating that neglected lattice distortion is occurred when $\mathrm{Pb}$ is replaced by $\mathrm{Sn}$ [21]. Therefore, $\mathrm{Pb/Sn}$ intermixed and pure Sn-based perovskite solar cells were studied [22–26]. By adjusting the ratio of $\mathrm{Pb}$ atoms to Sn atoms, the bandgap of the $\mathbf{MAPb}_{x}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}_{1-x}\mathbf{I}_{3}$ can be adjusted between 1.55 and $1.17\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 3a) and the $\mathbf{MAPb}_{x}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}_{1-x}\mathbf{I}_{3}$ achieve the lowest bandgap when the ratios of Sn are $50\%$ and $75\%$ [25]. By adjusting the $x$ value in $\mathbf{MAPb}_{x}\mathbf{S}\mathbf{n}_{1-x}\mathbf{I}_{3}$ , the energy transfer occurs in the valence band (VB) and conduction band (CB) of the perovskite films (Fig. 3b) [25]. Meanwhile, according to the absorption and PL spectra shown in Fig. 3c and 3d, when the content of Sn in $({\mathrm{FASnI}}_{3})_{1-x}({\mathrm{MAPbI}}_{3})_{x}$ film is $60\%$ , the bandgap of the film is the lowest [24]. However, since Sn2? is easily oxidized to $S\mathfrak{n}^{4+}$ in the air, the Sn contained perovskite materials are inferior to the $\mathrm{Pb}$ -based ones. Hence, it is still a challenge work to obtain a low-bandgap perovskite material with excellent performance and stability.
## 3.2 Low-bang gap organic materials
The low-band organic active layer in IPOSCs is responsible for absorbing NIR light and transferring charges. To achieve broad absorption of OSCs, designing and synthesizing low-bandgap donor materials is another effective method. A good donor polymer is composed of an electronrich donor group alternating with an electron-deficient acceptor group along its polymer backbone, namely a donor–acceptor (D-A) polymer. The D-A polymer can effectively adjust the bandgap, energy level, and carrier transport performance [27]. The polymer PCPDTBT synthesized using benzothiadiazole (BT) as an electron-deficient group has an optical bandgap of $1.4\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 4a) [28]. By adjusting the morphology of the film through additives, the PCE of PCPDTBT-based device increased from $3\%$ to $5.5\%$ [29]. The donor polymer PDTP-DFBT (Fig. 4b) was synthesized by introducing DTP group and combining with BT and showed an optical bandgap of $1.38~\mathrm{eV}$ . The PCE of the PDTP-DFBT based OSCs is $8\%$ and external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the device reaches $60\%$ in the NIR region [30]. Low-bandgap donor materials can also be obtained by using pyrrolopyrrole dione (DPP)-based polymer donors. The polymer donor material PDPP3T (Fig. 4c) and DPPTT-T synthesized by DPP all show good absorption in the NIR region [31, 32]. Subsequently, Ashraf et al. [33] synthesized a series of low-bandgap donor materials (C3-DPPTT-T-S, C3-DPPTT-T-Se and C3-DPPTT-T-Te) by copolymers with different chalcogenophene comonomers (thiophene, selenophene and tellurene) with DPP. The OSCs based on the chalcogenophene polymer series blended with $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ obtained PCE ranging from $7.1\%$ to $8.8\%$ and showed high photo-response in the NIR region. Besides, the DPP-based polymer PFDPPSe synthesized by Oklem et al. [34] has an absorption peak of $830~\mathrm{nm}$ and an absorption spectrum of $930~\mathrm{nm}$ , demonstrating good absorption of NIR light. The development of organic low-bandgap materials provides more options for the preparation of IPOSCs’ rear sub-cell.
In recent years, the low-bandgap non-fullerene acceptor (NFA) materials have developed rapidly. The PCE of reported single-junction and tandem OSCs based on NFA have exceeded $18\%$ and $17\%$ , respectively [35, 36]. The low-bandgap NFA is a molecule with D-A structure, and its conjugated main chain has strong electron donating groups and electron withdrawing groups, making it exhibit excellent light absorption in the NIR region [37]. In 2015, Zhan et al. designed a high-performance acceptor material ITIC with a strong push–pull effect in the molecule and prepared the OSCs realized a PCE of $6.8\%$ [38]. After that, in order to improve the utilization of NIR light, lowbandgap NFA with high absorption coefficient and strong infrared absorption has attracted widespread attention in the organic photovoltaic (OPV) research field. By adjusting the push–pull effect in the molecule, the bandgap and absorption spectrum of the material can be effectively adjusted [37]. Hou’s group designed the acceptor material IEICO (Fig. 4d) with an optical bandgap of $1.34\ \mathrm{eV}$ by modifying the end groups [39]. The absorption spectrum of
IEICO reached $925~\mathrm{nm}$ , which effectively increased the absorption of OSCs in the NIR region, and the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of OSCs reached $17.7\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Subsequently, they added fluorine (F) to the end group of IEICO and synthesized the acceptor material IEICO-4F (Fig. 4e). The optical bandgap of IEICO-4F is $1.24\ \mathrm{eV}$ and the absorption spectrum of IEICO-4F is extended to $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ , ensuring more NIR light to be used. Finally, the IEICO-4F based OSCs obtained a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $25.3\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and the PCE of the device reached $10.9\%$ [40]. After replacing fluorine (F) element with chlorine (Cl) element, a new acceptor material IEICO4Cl was synthesized (Fig. 4f). The absorption peak of IEICO-4Cl is at around $745{-}945~\mathrm{nm}$ and its absorption edge is extended to $1100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , make it a promising candidate for tandem solar cell [8]. Ding et al. designed a lowbandgap non-fullerene acceptor COi8DFIC with strong absorption in the NIR region. The OSCs prepared with PTB7-Th:COi8DFIC at a weight ratio of 1:1 obtained a
PCE of $12.16\%$ with a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $26.12\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ [41]. Later, ternary solar cells were prepared using this acceptor material, and the PCE of the device reached $14.08\%$ , and a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ up to $28.20\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was obtained [42]. Li et al. [43] reported a chlorinated non-fullerene acceptor BT-CIC with a bandgap of $1.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ and an absorption spectrum up to $1000~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The OSCs prepared by PTB7-Th:BT-CIC blend active layers achieved an average $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $22.5\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In 2019, Zou et al. used a ladder-shaped condensed ring (dithienothiophene[3.2-b]-pyrrolobenzothiazole) based on an electron-deficient core and a benzothiazole (BT) core to regulate the absorption and electron affinity of acceptor, the acceptor material Y6 was designed and synthesized. Owing to the higher absorption coefficient $(1.07\ \times\ 10^{5}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and wider absorption spectrum $(1.33\ \mathrm{eV})$ of Y6, the OSCs combined with the PM6 has obtained a PCE of $15.7\%$ [44]. Afterward, the alkyl chain of Y6 derivative BTP-4Cl-BO was optimized, and the acceptor material BTP-eC9 was obtained. Owing to the material’s wider absorption and better electron transport performance, the highest PCE of single-junction OSCs based on BTP-eC9 reached $17.8\%$ [45]. The Y6 and its derivative exhibited great potential in improving OSCs’ PCE.
## 4 Integrated perovskite/organic solar cells
## 4.1 IPOSCs based on low-bandgap donor
In 2014, Ding and Zuo [5] fabricated IPOSCs using a donor polymer poly (diketopyrrolopyrrole-terthiophene) (PDPP3T) whose bandgap is $0.22~\mathrm{eV}$ lower than that of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ , as the NIR absorbing material of the organic layer. The IPOSCs achieved PCE of $8.80\%$ , and the absorption range of IPOSCs was broadened to $970~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 5a, b). Zhang et al. [46] prepared the IPOSCs with the same low-bang-gap active layer, PDPP3T:PCBM, and the IPOSCs obtained a PCE of $8.92\%$ (Fig. 5c, d). Later, the low-bandgap donor PDPP3T was employed by Hou et al. to fabricated IPOSCs [47]. It was found that the optimal donor–acceptor weight ratio in OSCs cannot be directly applied to IPOSCs. The appropriate donor and acceptor weight ratio in active layer is helpful in forming better fiber and bi-continuous structure, which can provide more channels for electron transfer and enhance the device performance. The PCE of the IPOSCs with optimized donor and acceptor weight ratio finally reached $12.2\%$ (Fig. 5e). Meanwhile, a high NIR EQE over $40\%$ was obtained in IPOSCs (Fig. 5f).
Yang et al. fabricated IPOSCs utilized the low-bandgap donor materials DOR3T-TBDT and PBDTT-SeDPP [6]. Owing to the strong NIR absorption of PBDTT-SeDPP, the IPOSCs achieved a PCE of $12.0\%$ with increased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ to $20.6\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and extended photo-response to over $900~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 5g, h). However, although the DOR3TTBDT:PCBM integrated on $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ cannot broaden the photo-response of the device, the IPOSCs obtained enhanced $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and PCE of $21.2\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and $14.3\%$ , respectively. They also found that the integrated structure formed by perovskite and BHJ bi-layer is necessary to provide more photocurrent for the device, while the PerSCs using IR absorption material as the hole transport layer cannot employ the additional NIR light. In the subsequent research, Yang et al. demonstrated an efficient IPOSCs using PDTP-DFBT as NIR polymer donor in BHJ layer. Owing to the photogeneration in PDTP-DFBT:PCBM film, the photo-response of IPOSCs was extended to $900~\mathrm{nm}$ and a PCE of $15.8\%$ was obtained [12]. In 2016, Sun’s group synthesized two low-bandgap polymer materials M3 and M4 as NIR absorption materials for IPOSCs with the structure of $\mathrm{(FTO)/c\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/m\mathrm{-}T i O_{2}/(F A P b I_{3})_{0.85}(\mathrm{-}}$ $\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3})_{0.15}/B H J/V_{2}O_{5}/A u}$ [48]. When $\mathrm{PC}_{70}\mathrm{BM}$ is blended with M3 and M4, a BHJ layer is formed and integrated into the perovskite layer. The average PCE of $(\mathrm{FAPbI_{3}})_{0.85}(\mathrm{MAPbBr_{3}})_{0.15}/\mathrm{M3}{:}\mathrm{PC_{70}B M}$ and (FAP$\mathrm{\bI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}/M4;P C_{70}B M}$ IPOSCs are $16.2\%$ (reverse scan: $17.3\%$ , forward scan: $15.1\%$ ) and $15.0\%$ (reverse scan: $15.9\%$ , forward scan: $14.0\%$ ) (Fig. 5i), respectively, which are obviously superior to the PCE of $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}/M3}$ $(14.8\%)$ and (FAP$\mathrm{\bI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}/M4}$ $(12.3\%)$ PerSCs. The IPOSCs based on $\mathbf{M4:PC_{70}B M}$ BHJ layer realized extended monochromatic incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) response (Fig. 5j). Another low-bandgap donor DT-PDPP2T-TT (TT) was employed in IPOSCs by Kim et al. [49]. The ternary organic active layer blended by TT:PC71BM:N2200 was used to optimize the IPOSCs fabrication. The addition of N2200 combined with the solvent addition treatment of diphenyl ether (DPE) enables TT and $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ to form a uniform bi-continuous network and increases the electron mobility to $1\times10^{-2}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ . The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of IPOSCs prepared by N2200 and DPE synergistically increased significantly compared to that without N2200 and DPE treatment. Finally, the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ increased from $17.61\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of pure $\mathrm{PerSCs}$ to $20.04\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of IPOSCs (Fig. 5k). The increased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ was mainly due to the increased light absorption of the BHJ layer at the wavelength range of $800{-}920~\mathrm{{nm}}$ (Fig. 5l). Afterward, Daboczi et al. [50] prepared the IPOSCs applying the same TT polymer. Since the optimization of device interlayers, the PCE of the IPOSCs was promoted to $17.7\%$ with higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}},\:J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and EQE performance (Fig. 5m, n), compared to that of IPOSCs made by Kim et al. [49]. Gao et al. [51] synthesized a new porphyrin DPPZnP-TSEH and mix it with $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ as an effective BHJ for IPOSCs. They found that when $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ was added to DPPZnP-TSEH film and mixed to form BHJ layer, the film’s hole mobility was increased from $3.2\times10^{-5}~\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (DPPZnP-TSEH) to $1.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{cm}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{V}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (DPPZnP-TSEH: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}=$ 4:1). Finally, the IPOSCs prepared with optimized DPPZnP-TSEH and $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ weight ratio obtained the highest PCE of $19.02\%$ with $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.32\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and high fill factor (FF) of $78.4\%$ (Fig. 5o, p). The detail photovoltaic parameters of these abovementioned IPOSCs are listed in Table 1 [5, 6, 12, 46–51].
## 4.2 IPOSCs based on low-bandgap acceptor
Owing to the non-fullerene acceptor (NFA) show strong absorption in NIR region, we introduced NFA IEICO to prepare IPOSCs [52]. By depositing PBDTTT-E-T:IEICO on $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ , the photo-response of IPOSCs was extended to $930~\mathrm{nm}$ and effective charge transfer was demonstrated between $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and PBDTTTET:IEICO (Fig. 6b). Finally, the integrated current density $(J_{\mathrm{int}})$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}/$ PBDTTT-ET:IEICO IPOSCs reached $23.8\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , much higher than that of the pure PerSCs based on $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(20.4~\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ ) (Fig. 6a). Moreover, the IPOSCs achieved high NIR EQE of more than $50\%$ at $800{-}930~\mathrm{nm}$ . In addition, by preparing PBDTTT-E-T and IEICO as hole transport layers on $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ , respectively, the PerSCs obtained efficiencies of $10.46\%$ and $8.85\%$ , which can be concluded that both donor and acceptor have hole transport properties in these IPOSCs. To further improve the performance of
IPOSCs, we proposed to control the distribution of donors and acceptors in the BHJ layer to facilitate the charge transportation and decrease the charge recombination [53]. By optimizing the weight ratio of donor (PBDB-T) and acceptor (IEICO) to suppress the concentration gradient distribution of donor and acceptor in the vertical direction, a continuous and uniform interpenetrating network of BHJ layer was constructed. The distribution of donors and acceptors in the BHJ layer was detected by time-of-flight secondary-ion mass spectroscopy (TOF– SIMS). Owing to the good donor distribution provides a good transport channel for the holes generated in the perovskite layer and suppresses the recombination of carriers, the optimized IPOCSs achieved a PCE of $15.47\%$ (Fig. 6c, d). Owing to the lager bandgap of all-inorganic PerSCs limited the PCE increasing of all-inorganic PerSCs, the all-inorganic/organic IPOSCs can effectively utilize the higher voltage of the all-inorganic perovskite and the BHJ layer’s absorption of near-infrared light, thereby improving the efficiency of the device. We designed $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}/\mathrm{BHJ}$ IPOSCs using $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ as the ultraviolet–visible light absorption layer and PBDTTT-ET:IEICO as the NIR light absorption layer [54]. The IPOSCs achieved similar $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ with $\mathrm{PerSCs}$ and the additional absorption of the BHJ layer increases the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ from $14.78\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (PerSCs) to $15.98\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (IPOSCs). Finally, the PCE of IPOSCs reached $14.03\%$ , which was higher than that of PerSCs $(12.53\%)$ (Fig. 6e, f). In addition to $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ , the all-inorganic perovskite $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ was also used in IPOSCs. Zhao et al. [55] fabricated a $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathbf{B}}\mathrm{r}_{3}/\mathrm{J}61$ :ITIC IPOSCs to extend the optical absorption of inorganic $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ and the optimal IPOSCs achieved a PCE of $10.34\%$ with extended EQE response (Fig. 6g, h). Chen et al. [56] introduced ternary BHJ PBDB-TF:BT-CIC: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ as ETL to fabricate IPOSCs and unveiled that an appropriate third component in BHJ can enable the ETL to form better cascadeenergy-level alignment and increase the hole mobility. By optimizing the morphology of this BHJ layer and the vertical distribution of the donor and acceptor, the IPOSCs using $\mathrm{(FAPbI_{3})_{0.85}(M A P b B r_{3})_{0.15}}$ as perovskite layer and PBDB-TF:BT-CIC: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ ternary system as BHJ layer achieved a PCE of $18\%$ with extended optical response to $950~\mathrm{nm}$ and NIR EQE of $40\%$ at $800~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6i, j). Recently, Zhang et al. [57] constructed IPOSCs employed fused-ring electron acceptor (F8IC) with strong NIR absorption and high electron mobility to blend with a narrow-bandgap polymer donor. The IPOSCs exhibit photo-response to $1000~\mathrm{{nm}}$ with EQE near $80\%$ in the NIR region and achieve an ultrahigh $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $28.2\ \mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (Fig. 6k, l). In the last two years, the Y6 series materials have been widely applied in efficient OSCs. Recently, Y6 has been succeeded utilized in IPOSCs. Chen et al. [13] and Liao et al. [58] constructed IPOSCs by integrated $\mathrm{S1{:}P C_{61}B M{:}Y6}$ [13] and ${\mathrm{S}}2{\mathrm{:}}{\mathrm{PC}}_{61}{\mathrm{BM:Y}}6$ [58] on perovskite layer, respectively. The two IPOSCs utilizing Y6 as NIR absorber achieved high $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and NIR EQE response (Fig. 6n, p). Finally, the IPOSCs based on $\mathrm{S1{:}P C_{61}B M{:}Y6}$ and $\mathrm{S}2{:}\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM:Y}6$ organic active layer obtained PCE of $16.65\%$ and $20.61\%$ (Fig. 6m, o), demonstrating that the Y6 series materials have potential to enhance the NIR utilization and device performance of IPOSCs. The highest NIR EQE of $\sim90\%$ at $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ was obtained for IPOSCs. The detailed photovoltaic parameters of these IPOSCs are summarized in Table 1 [13, 52–58].
## 4.3 Charge dynamics of IPOSCs
Owing to the absorption of NIR light by low-bandgap organic active layer, IPOSCs have achieved a great improvement in $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , compared to $\mathrm{PerSCs}$ . However, the highest PCE of IPOSCs was still inferior to that of the high-efficient PerSCs, which can ascribe to the relatively low $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF. In IPOSCs, the charge dynamics, including charge separation, transport and recombination, always greatly affects the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF of the devices.
In order to investigated the charge generation and transportation in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ or PDPP3T:PCBM in the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ /PDPP3T:PCBM IPOSCs, the voltage-biased EQE measurement and $J_{\neg}V$ curves under monochromatic light irradiation were conducted by Zhang et al. [46]. The voltage-biased EQE measurement manifested that the EQE spectra in the range from 780 to $950~\mathrm{nm}$ dropped gradually to zero with the bias voltage increased from 0 to $0.64\mathrm{~V~}$ and the whole EQE spectra were decreased to zero under $0.82\mathrm{~V~}$ bias voltage (Fig. 7a). These results reflect that the charge generated in perovskite and BHJ layer behave independently in IPOSCs, similar to what observed in ternary OSCs. When the IPOSCs was under 525 or $825~\mathrm{nm}$ monochromatic light irradiation, the patterns of the $J_{-}V$ curves under 525 and $850~\mathrm{nm}$ monochromatic light irradiation appear to be very similar to the combination of each independent $J_{\cdot}V$ curve (Fig. 7b) and the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of IPOSCs was consistent with that of IPOSCs illuminated with single $525~\mathrm{nm}$ monochromatic light as well as the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of PerSCs. This indicates that the IPOSCs were a parallel-like tandem system. Hence, the charge carriers generated either in perovskite or BHJ layer retain their individual electronic properties and transport independently [46].
Afterward, in order to reveal the reason that the high voltage of the integrated device is dominated by the perovskite layer instead of the organic layer, Dong et al. [12] analyzed the high voltage based on the bandgap structure and proposed a quasi-Fermi level pinning model (Fig. 8). They found that the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of IPOSCs is determined by the quasi-Fermi level splitting of the double absorber layer system, while the quasi-Fermi level splitting of the double absorber layer system is fixed on the perovskite layer. The success of quasi-Fermi level splitting and pinning should be attributed to the uniqueness of this perovskite/BHJ double-layer structure and the matching energy level between the polymer, fullerene derivative and perovskite. Therefore, the integrated device can maintain a high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ in the case of additional collection of NIR light. This study shows that it is expected to improve the device performance of IPOSCs by combining a more efficient perovskite layer with BHJ with broad spectral absorption.
Owing to that the perovskite and organic active layers not only capture light to generate free charge carrier, but also collect the charge carrier and transport charge carrier, the charge transportation is more complicatedly in IPOSCs, compare to the single-junction PerSCs and OSCs. Dong et al. [12] investigated the charge carrier transfer between the perovskite and BHJ layer using steady-state photoluminescence (PL) and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) (Fig. 9a, b). The PL quenching of perovskite and TRPL decay in perovskite/BHJ film indicates faster charge carrier transfer from perovskite into the BHJ film. In addition to PL and TRPL, they also studied the carrier dynamics along the entire pathway in IPOSCs by laser-induced transient photocurrent (TPC) (Fig. 9c) and transient photovoltage (TPV) (Fig. 9d) technique. By comparing the conventional PerSCs using PCBM as ETL, the IPOSCs with $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3-x}C l_{\it x}/P D T I}$ -DFBT:PCBM bi-active layer show shorter photocurrent decay lifetime and longer photovoltage decay lifetime. These results indicate that the electron carrier extraction ability of PDTP-DFBT:PCBM layer is better than that of PCBM layer, and the charge recombination rate in IPOSCs is lower than that in PerSCs.
The PL quenching of perovskite in perovskite/BHJ film was also detected in our reported work, which reflected the fast charge transfer between perovskite and BHJ layer. However, the PL corresponding to low-bandgap organic materials (IEICO) was enhanced, which can be ascribed to the charge recombination in IEICO as well as the absorption of PL fluorescence by PBDTTT-E-T:IEICO layer. To accurately detect the charge-transfer process in the $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}/ $ PBDTTT-E-T:IEICO integrated film, we performed the photoinduced transient absorption spectroscopy (TAS) measurement (Fig. 10a, b) [54]. According to the TAS signals’ changes at delay time of 2 and 800 ps related to the feature absorption of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ , PBDTTT-E-T and IEICO, the charge transfer between $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\mathrm{Br}/\mathrm{P}$ BDTTT-ET, $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}\mathrm{Br/IECIO}$ and PBDTTT-E-T/IEICO was confirmed to exist simultaneously. The transmission of hole from $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{I}}_{2}\mathrm{Br}$ to IECIO and PBDTTT-E-T evidence that the PBDTTT-E-T and IECIO can collect and transport the hole from perovskite to electrode, which was different from common sense that donor transporting hole and acceptor transporting electrons in conventional OSCs. The results shed that choice of donor and acceptor of BHJ layer has a great influence on the charge-transfer dynamic process in
IPOSCs. Moreover, in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ /PBDTTT-E-T:IEICO IPOSCs, the hole from perovskite layer would recombine with the electron in IEICO transferred, resulting in an S-shape in $J_{-}V$ curves as well as the decrease of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and FF (Fig. 6a) [52].
The TAS measurement was also used by Gao et al. [51] and Daboczi et al. [50] to investigate the charge-transfer dynamic in IPOSCs. The apparent superposition signal at $750~\mathrm{nm}$ of PB2 in perovskite/BHJ layer (Fig. 10c) at early stage (2 ps) exhibits that both perovskite and BHJ are simultaneously excited below the wavelength of $800~\mathrm{nm}$ and the BHJ keeping excited and being photoconductive during operation makes it can facilitate the charge transfer. A slight decrement of signal at $750~\mathrm{nm}$ in perovskite/BHJ than perovskite verifies that an ultrafast transfer of charge carriers at the interface between perovskite and BHJ, which is consistent with our result (Fig. 10a). Owing to an additional decay channel was introduced when the BHJ layer was integrated on perovskite films, the signal intensity decay of perovskite/ BHJ was slight faster than that of perovskite (Fig. 10d). As the kinetic traces at $870~\mathrm{nm}$ and the kinetic traces of ground state bleaching below the bandgap of perovskite (Fig. 10f), the positive PIA (perovskite) and the sharp decay $\left(<2\right.$ ps) toward the appearance of negative (DPPZnP
TSEH: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM},$ as well as the slower repopulation in perovskite/DPPZnP-TSEH: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ than simple DPPZnPTSEH: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ blend was observed in perovskite/DPPZnPTSEH: $\mathrm{PC}_{61}\mathrm{BM}$ . This information confirmed that the charge was rapidly transfer from perovskite to BHJ $\left(<2\right.$ ps) and repopulated the BHJ besides polaron generation in BHJ itself. Through the TAS measurement and the data analysis (shown in Fig. $10\mathrm{g}$ ), Daboczi et al. [50] also confirmed the charge transfer between $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ and TT:PCBM:N2200. In addition, the TAS data shows that the direction of hole transfer from MAPI to TT is the reverse of that required in complete IPOSCs. The charge accumulation in the complete IPOSCs at the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/T T}$ would causing undesired charge recombination, thereby resulting in the decrease of FF.
In IPOSCs, the thickness of the BHJ layer also has an important influence on device performance. The BHJ layer should have sufficient thickness to harvest NIR light, while a too thick BHJ layer may cause extra charge recombination, leading to PCE decrease. Ding and Zuo [5] studied the performance of IPOSCs by increasing the thickness of PDPP3T:PCBM from 45 to $130~\mathrm{nm}$ . As the thickness of the BHJ increases, the photo-response gradually decreases in the absorption range of the perovskite layer and gradually increases in the near-infrared range beyond $800~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . To optimize the device performance of IPOSCs, Kim et al. [49] tuned the thickness of BHJ layer from 60 to $170~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . According to their research, IPOSCs with $\Gamma\mathrm{T}\mathrm{:PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ BHJ layer achieve the best performance when the thickness of BHJ layer is $60~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in that although the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of IPOSCs was increased as the thickness of the BHJ increases, the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF was decreased. When the N2200 was added in BHJ layer, due to the improvement of the morphology of the BHJ layer, the electron mobility was increased and the charge recombination caused by the increase in thickness was decreased. Therefore, the PCE of IPOSCs with $\mathrm{TT}{\mathrm{:PC}}_{71}\mathrm{BM:N}2200\mathrm{BH}.$ J layer was improved to $16.36\%$ as the thickness of BHJ increases from 60 to $100~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . In addition, we found that the optimized thickness of the BHJ layer in $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3^{\prime}}}$ /PBDTTT-E-T:IEICO IPOSCs ( $\mathrm{130~nm})$ was different from that in CsPbI Br/PBDTTT-ET:IEICO IPOSCs $(90~\mathrm{nm})$ [52, 54]. This result reveals that the influence of different types of perovskite on charge transport will also determine the optimal thickness of BHJ layer. In order to achieve more light capture and lower charge recombination, the thickness-insensitive and efficient low-bandgap BHJ layer with high charge mobility will be a great candidate for efficient IPOSCs.
## 5 Summary and outlook
In summary, IPOSCs combining perovskite and lowbandgap organic active layer are promising to enhance NIR light employment. IPOSCs feature a high voltage given by perovskite materials and a broad photo-response given by low-bandgap organic materials, making it expected to approach or exceed the Shockley–Queisser limit of singlejunction solar cells. In the last few years, IPOSCs have made great progress in improving the application of NIR light. Various low-bandgap materials with strong absorption in NIR region have been successfully employed in IPOSCs. The IPOSCs have shown obvious advantages in increasing the current of the PerSCs and exhibits great potential for the improvement of the efficiency of the PerSCs. However, compared with high-efficiency PerSCs, the voltage loss of IPOSCs is still much higher. Hence, although a significant improvement in the short-circuit current of IPOSCs was realized, the highest PCE of IPOSCs is still lower than that of PerSCs. The charge recombination during the transfer process is an important reason for the large voltage loss of IPOSCs. In the future research of IPOSCs, to further promote the device performance of IPOSCs, it is urgent to study the charge dynamic in the IPOSCs. A deeper understanding of the dynamic process of charge generation, separation, transmission and recombination is of vital importance for researchers to take effective measures to reduce voltage loss and increase FF. At present, the low-bandgap acceptor Y6 has been proved to enable IPOSCs to achieve PCE over $20\%$ . In the next stage, synthesis of low-bandgap materials with higher mobility, stronger near-infrared absorption, and better energy level alignment for IPOSCs is a reliable method to improve the PCE of IPOSCs. It is ensured that the IPOSCs not only greatly improves the utilization of near-infrared light, but also has low voltage loss, so that it can obtain a higher PCE than the single-junction PerSCs. In addition, according to the charge dynamic characteristics of IPOSCs, it is also necessary to carry out targeted optimization from the perspective of device preparation. Nowadays, interface engineering, morphology engineering and vertical component engineering have been successfully applied to optimize the charge transfer in IPOSCs. Since the perovskite is contact directly with the donor and acceptor, to avoid the charge accumulation at the perovskite/BHJ interface, further optimization of the perovskite/BHJ interface may be very helpful for the IPOSCs realizing high PCE.
